---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="About - Paul Ann Labs"
  description="An AI application company built from inside operations, not from a conference room."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 py-20">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold">About</h1>

    <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-slate">
      Paul Ann Labs deploys AI inside the operating core of a business ... the systems that
      run payroll, safety, dispatch, maintenance, purchasing. The unglamorous machinery
      that never appears in a marketing report but decides whether a company works.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Where the methodology came from</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-slate">
      Strangler Fig wasn't designed in a conference room. It came out of doing the work ...
      applying AI to real operations where a bad decision had consequences that showed up
      the same week.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-slate">
      That produced a specific conviction: small and mid-sized businesses will not replace
      systems that currently work, and they're right not to. Every methodology that starts
      with migration is solving the consultant's problem, not the owner's. So the approach
      had to grow around what exists instead of demanding its removal.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Why an internal owner, always</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-slate">
      Most AI deployments don't fail technically. They fail because the person who
      understood the system left, or never worked there in the first place. So the audit
      identifies a <strong class="text-navy">Stem</strong> ... someone already inside the
      company, frequently not the person with the relevant title ... and the engagement is
      structured so that person ends up owning it.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-slate">
      Peppermint exists for the same reason. Capability that walks out the door with a
      consultant was never capability.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">The family</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-slate">
      Paul Ann Labs sits under <strong class="text-navy">Trimmie Holdings</strong>, alongside
      <a href="https://paulannmedia.com" class="text-fig hover:underline">Paul Ann Media</a>.
      Media handles demand ... audience, brand, pipeline. Labs handles operations. Most
      businesses need one before the other, and we're direct about which.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-slate">
      The names come from a family. Trimmie from two first names, Paul Ann from the middle
      ones. It seemed like the right way to sign work you intend to stand behind.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">How we work</h2>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-slate">
      <li><strong class="text-navy">Small first.</strong> One process, one month, visible output before anything bigger.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-navy">Reversible.</strong> Every phase can be undone. That's what makes it safe to start.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-navy">Hand it over.</strong> The goal is your Stem running it, not a permanent retainer.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-navy">Stop when it's enough.</strong> Most engagements shouldn't reach Phase Five.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="mt-16 rounded-lg border border-navy/10 bg-white p-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Start Phase One</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        One process. One month. Tell us where the friction is.
      </p>
      <a href="mailto:hello@paulannlabs.com"
         class="mt-5 inline-block rounded bg-fig px-6 py-3 font-semibold text-navy hover:bg-fig-light transition-colors">
        hello@paulannlabs.com
      </a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
